LESSONS_S2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The item's selling side: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module covers items and price lists from the selling side in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the item's commercial field set; configure units of measure and sales units with their conversions; design the price list architecture; maintain Item Price records by governed update; build Pricing Rules for quantity breaks and structured discounts; verify the item-side tax fields; and run the pricing reads.</p><p><b>One record, several owners.</b> The Item is one record read by several functions, and each function owns its section: the Inventory track owns the stock flags, defaults, and identity settings (Inventory modules 1 and 3); the Accounts track owns the account derivations and tax correctness; this module owns the commercial section — how the item names, sells, converts, and prices. The one-record principle means the selling side never creates a second record for a product that exists; it completes the commercial section of the record the register already holds, through the item creation gate the Inventory track defined, so one product always has one history across stock and sales.</p><p><b>What selling reads from the Item.</b></p><p>1. <b>The identity fields</b> — code, name, description, image: what prints on quotations, orders, and invoices.<br>2. <b>The item group</b> — the classification driving sales reporting, tax and account derivations, and pricing rule scopes.<br>3. <b>The units</b> — the stock unit and the sales units with conversion factors (Chapter 3): what quantity means on a document.<br>4. <b>The prices</b> — through the price lists and Item Price records (Chapters 4-5), and the Pricing Rules layered above them (Chapter 6).<br>5. <b>The tax classification</b> — the item-side field the tax machinery reads (Chapter 7; module 6 covers the machinery).</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The sales function maintains the commercial section; price changes move only by the governed update procedure (Chapter 5); pricing rules are register entries with owners and reasons (Chapter 6); and the module's standing rule follows the programme's pattern: <b>a price is a decision with a record, never a figure typed at document time.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the commercial field walk. Chapter 3: units of measure and sales units. Chapter 4: the price list architecture. Chapter 5: Item Price maintenance. Chapter 6: Pricing Rules. Chapter 7: item tax fields. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The commercial field walk", "12", "<p>Open the Item record (created through the Inventory track's gate). Complete the commercial fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Item Code.</b> Per the written coding convention — short, unique, structured (PARA-500 for Paracetamol 500mg). The code is the register's handle: it prints on documents, keys every report, and is typed and scanned daily. Codes are permanent; a product whose code must change is retired and re-created through the lifecycle procedures, because documents reference codes forever.</p><p><b>2. Item Name and Description.</b> The name as customers should read it on documents — <i>Paracetamol 500mg Tablets</i> — complete, consistently formatted, without internal abbreviations customers will not recognise. The description field carries the longer print text where documents need it. Both print exactly as entered; the verification is reading a draft document, not the form.</p><p><b>3. Item Group.</b> The classification from the item group tree — analgesics, antibiotics, consumables, per the register's design. The group is a working field on the selling side: sales reports aggregate by it, tax and account derivations can key on it, and Pricing Rules scope by it (Chapter 6). The tree follows the standing design rules: classes the business treats differently, three levels or fewer, changes by decision.</p><p><b>4. The unit fields.</b> The Stock UOM (the unit balances count in — the Inventory track's decision) and the sales-side unit configuration Chapter 3 covers. The commercial walk confirms them; it does not change them casually, because unit changes are posting-behaviour changes under the lifecycle's change governance.</p><p><b>5. Is Sales Item.</b> The flag that admits the item to selling documents. Items the business stocks but does not sell (consumables, spares) leave it off; the flag is the clean answer to why an item does not appear in a document picker.</p><p><b>6. The sales defaults.</b> Per company where used: the default income account and cost center derivations the Accounts track governs — confirmed present per the accountant's design, because a missing derivation surfaces as an accounting question on the first invoice.</p><p><b>7. Image.</b> Where the business prints or displays catalogues and the POS uses images, the image field per the site's convention.</p><p><b>Verification per item.</b> The code per convention; the name as a customer should read it; the group per the tree; the units confirmed; Is Sales Item correct; the defaults present; then one draft quotation row — the code, name, unit, and price appearing as intended — deleted after reading.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Units of measure and sales units", "12", "<p>Customers buy in different units of the same product. This chapter is the unit machinery.</p><p><b>1. The UOM records.</b> Open UOM (awesome bar): the unit master — Unit, Carton-12, Box, Pack. Units are shared records created once per the register's naming; a new unit is a register decision, not a per-item improvisation, because Carton-12 and Ctn-12 as separate records split conversion history.</p><p><b>2. The item's conversion table.</b> On the Item, the UOM conversion table: one row per alternative unit with its factor — <i>Carton-12 = 12 units</i> where the stock unit is the single unit. The rules:</p><p>1. <b>The factor states how many stock units one alternative unit contains.</b> Every document row entered in an alternative unit converts to stock units by this factor for all stock and value arithmetic.<br>2. <b>Factors come from the physical product</b>, verified at the register (the supplier's carton contents counted, not assumed) — a wrong factor mis-converts every document that uses the unit, and the Inventory track's UoM error family (the carton-rate-against-unit-line receipt, the fractional batch balances) is this table wrong or misread.<br>3. <b>Factor changes are governed events:</b> a supplier changing carton contents is a dated change with the affected prices reviewed (Chapter 5), because the price per carton and the factor move together or the per-unit price silently changes.</p><p><b>3. The Sales UOM.</b> The item's default selling unit — the unit document rows pre-fill with. Set it to how the majority channel buys; rows can select any converted unit per transaction. The standing retail-and-trade pattern: retail counters buy by the unit; chains and institutions buy by the carton — one item record serving both through the conversion table, with the price architecture (Chapters 4-6) carrying the price per unit and per carton per list.</p><p><b>4. Units on documents.</b> The document row carries the quantity, the unit, and the conversion — and prints the unit the customer ordered in. The operator's rules:</p><p>1. <b>The unit column is read, not assumed:</b> 50 on a row means 50 of the row's unit; the order for 50 cartons entered as 50 units under-ships by the factor, and the checking discipline on orders and deliveries (modules 4-5) names the unit column explicitly for this reason.<br>2. <b>Prices follow the unit</b> (Chapter 5's per-UoM prices where used): the rate on the row is per the row's unit, and the row amount is quantity times rate in that unit — readable arithmetic on every row.</p><p><b>5. The conversion read.</b> Quarterly, riding the item lifecycle reads: conversion factors sampled against physical products for the top-selling items; new units created in the quarter checked against the register; items selling in units their table does not carry (a workaround pattern) corrected by adding the decided unit rather than by per-document arithmetic.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The price list architecture", "12", "<p>Prices live on lists; customers resolve to a list. This chapter is the architecture.</p><p><b>1. The Price List record.</b> Open Price List (awesome bar) &gt; New: the name, the currency, and the Selling flag (buying lists belong to the Procurement track). A price list is a named pricing context — a set of Item Prices (Chapter 5) that applies to the customers who resolve to it.</p><p><b>2. The standing architecture.</b> Design the list set from the commercial classes module 1's Customer Group tree defined — the lists and the groups are the same design read from two sides:</p><p>1. <b>Standard Selling (retail)</b> — the counter and default prices.<br>2. <b>Trade</b> — the chain, institution, and distributor prices.<br>3. <b>Additional lists only where a class genuinely prices differently as a class</b> — a list per customer is an anti-pattern (customer-specific arrangements are Pricing Rules or negotiated document prices with their approvals, not private lists), because every list is a full price set to maintain, and the maintenance cost of Chapter 5 multiplies by the list count.</p><p><b>3. The resolution, restated.</b> Module 1 wired it: the document's price list resolves from the customer's default, else the customer group's, else the selling default. The consequence for this module: <b>putting a customer in the right group is a pricing act.</b> The classification read (module 1, Chapter 3) is therefore also a pricing control, and the wrong-group customer is the wrong-list customer.</p><p><b>4. Currency rules.</b> Each list carries one currency; a customer billing in another currency needs a list in that currency or the exchange-rate machinery the Accounts track governs applied at document time per the site's configuration. For the standing Nigeria-first case: naira lists, with foreign-currency arrangements as the documented exception they are.</p><p><b>5. What price lists are not.</b></p><p>1. <b>Not discounts:</b> a percentage relationship between lists (trade at a margin below retail) is maintained by the update procedure's arithmetic, not by a discount mechanism — the list states the price, and the document shows the price, which keeps document reading simple and disputes short.<br>2. <b>Not history:</b> superseded prices are history the Item Price records and the update files carry (Chapter 5); the list always states the current price.<br>3. <b>Not per-document overrides:</b> the negotiated price on one order is that document's rate with its approval per module 4's rules; the list does not move for one order.</p><p><b>6. The architecture read.</b> Annually, with the pricing review: the list set against the customer groups (one behavioural class, one list), orphan lists (no customers resolve to them) retired, and the resolution sampled — five customers per group, a draft document each, the pre-filled list confirmed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Item Price maintenance", "12", "<p>An Item Price is one item's price on one list. This chapter is the record and the governed update.</p><p><b>1. The Item Price record.</b> Open Item Price (awesome bar): the item, the price list, the rate, and — where the site uses them — the UOM the rate applies to, minimum quantity, and validity dates. The standing shape:</p><p>1. <b>One row per item per list</b> for the base case: PARA-500 on Standard Selling at the unit rate; PARA-500 on Trade at the trade rate.<br>2. <b>Per-UoM rows where the same list prices units and cartons separately</b> — the unit rate and the Carton-12 rate as two rows on the same list, each applying when the document row's unit matches. The carton rate and the conversion factor are maintained together (Chapter 3's rule).<br>3. <b>Validity dates where prices are scheduled</b> — the dated price change entered in advance, effective automatically.</p><p><b>2. The governed update procedure.</b> Prices change by decision, on a cycle, through one procedure:</p><p>1. <b>The decision</b> — the pricing review (the owner's cadence: monthly or per supplier cost events) produces the approved price file: item, list, old rate, new rate, effective date, and the basis (the cost change, the margin decision — module 5 of Inventory supplies the cost side; the margin arithmetic is the accountant's review).<br>2. <b>The load</b> — the approved file applied by governed import or entry, by the roles the matrix permits, on the effective date or with validity dates.<br>3. <b>The verification</b> — a sample of updated items drafted on documents per list, rates confirmed; the file and verification filed with the review.</p><p><b>3. Why the procedure is strict.</b> The price fields are among the most financially consequential editable figures in the system: a wrong rate prices every document until noticed, and an unauthorised change is a margin leak with a login attached. The controls follow: price-write permission held narrowly per the matrix; the monthly price changes read (below) listing every Item Price change against the approved files; and no price typed at document time except as the negotiated, approved document rate module 4 governs.</p><p><b>4. The price changes read — monthly.</b> Every Item Price created or modified in the month, with who and when, reconciled against the approved price files. Changes without a file are corrected and their permission gap closed — the same fields-move-only-by-decision audit as the credit changes read, applied to prices.</p><p><b>5. The completeness read — monthly.</b> Sales items without a current price on their group's list (the picker will fetch nothing, and the document will demand a typed rate — the exact door the module keeps closed); expired validity rows whose successors were never loaded. Both lists worked to zero with the update cycle.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Pricing Rules", "13", "<p>Structured price behaviour — quantity breaks, class discounts, promotions — is configured as Pricing Rules, not typed as document discounts. This chapter is the doctype and its register.</p><p><b>1. The Pricing Rule doctype.</b> Open Pricing Rule (awesome bar) &gt; New. The record in three parts:</p><p>1. <b>The scope — what it applies to:</b> item, item group, or brand; and for whom — customer, customer group, or territory. The scope fields make the rule as narrow or broad as the decision: one item for one customer group, or a whole group for all trade customers.<br>2. <b>The condition — when it applies:</b> minimum (and maximum) quantity or amount thresholds, and validity dates. Quantity thresholds are the quantity-break mechanism: <i>from 50 units, the rate becomes ₦950</i> is a rule with min qty 50.<br>3. <b>The effect — what it does:</b> a rate (replacing the list price), a discount percentage, or a discount amount, applied per the rule's price-or-discount configuration.</p><p><b>2. Priority and interaction.</b> Where multiple rules could apply to one row, the priority field and the rule settings decide which wins; the site's standing convention: rules are designed not to overlap, and where an overlap is intended (a promotion superseding a standing break), the priority is set explicitly and recorded. An undesigned overlap prices documents by accident, and the register (below) exists to make every rule's scope visible before a new one is added.</p><p><b>3. Pricing Rules versus the other pricing instruments — the decision table.</b></p><p>1. <b>The price list</b> — the standing price per class: who you are decides the list.<br>2. <b>The Pricing Rule</b> — structured, conditional behaviour on top of the list: how much or when you buy changes the price, by a written rule with dates.<br>3. <b>The document-level discount or negotiated rate</b> — the one-off commercial decision on one document, with its approval per module 4's rules.</p><p>Each instrument leaves its own evidence, and the separation exists for that reason: the list states the standing position, the rule states the standing condition, and the document shows the exception with its approver.</p><p><b>4. The pricing rules register.</b> One table, per the standing register pattern: each active rule with its scope, condition, effect, owner, reason, and validity — reviewed at the pricing review. Rules without owners or with lapsed reasons are disabled at the review; promotions carry end dates at creation so lapsing is automatic rather than remembered.</p><p><b>5. The rules read — monthly.</b> New and changed rules against the register; expired promotions confirmed inactive; and the effect sample — three documents drafted inside rule conditions (the 50-unit row confirming ₦950) and three outside them (the 49-unit row confirming the list rate), verifying the machinery prices as the register says it should.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Item tax fields", "12", "<p>Tax computation is module 6's subject; this chapter is the item's side of it — the classification the machinery reads.</p><p><b>1. The item tax template field.</b> On the Item (or derived from its item group per the site's design): the Item Tax Template that states the item's tax treatment — standard-rated or exempt, per the tax categories the accountant defines. For a Nigerian pharmaceutical register the distinction is live: many pharmaceutical items are VAT-exempt while general goods are standard-rated, and the item-side field is where each product's treatment is stated once.</p><p><b>2. Why the field lives on the item.</b> The document's tax computation (module 6) combines the document-level tax template with the item-level treatment: a mixed basket — exempt pharmaceuticals and standard-rated consumables on one invoice — taxes correctly per line because each item carries its own classification. The alternative (per-document tax editing) taxes whole documents by operator memory, which is a compliance error generator.</p><p><b>3. Setting the classification.</b></p><p>1. <b>By class, at the gate:</b> the item's tax treatment follows its class per the accountant's ruling table — the item group or the class row states the template, and the gate applies it at creation with the other class-row settings (the Inventory track's control register pattern extended to tax).<br>2. <b>Changes are governed:</b> a reclassification (a product's exemption status changing by regulation) is a dated decision from the accountant, applied through the change procedure, with the effective date recorded — because tax treatment changes are exactly the changes an auditor asks about.</p><p><b>4. The item tax read — quarterly, with the accountant.</b> The register sampled by class: exempt classes carrying exempt templates, standard classes carrying standard ones, exceptions listed and resolved. New items created in the quarter checked against the ruling table. The read is the selling side's contribution to the tax compliance the Accounts track owns; module 6 adds the document-side reads.</p><p><b>5. The boundary, stated.</b> The selling function applies the accountant's rulings; it does not make them. A customer's request to be invoiced without tax, an item whose treatment seems wrong, a new product class without a ruling — each routes to the accountant as a question, and the document waits for the ruling rather than improvising one. The item tax field carries a decided answer or it carries nothing; there is no operator-judgment lane in tax classification.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade's selling architecture", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to HealthTrade's item and pricing build. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the commercial section completed (Chapter 2).</b> The register's items — created through the Inventory gate — receive their commercial walk: codes per the convention (<b>PARA-500</b>), names as customers read them (<b>Paracetamol 500mg Tablets</b>), groups per the tree (Analgesics under Pharmaceuticals), Is Sales Item confirmed, defaults per the accountant's design. The draft-row verification runs per class sample.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the unit set (Chapter 3).</b> The two-channel reality is configured on one record: stock unit <i>Unit</i>; the conversion table carrying <b>Carton-12 = 12 units</b>, the factor verified against the physical carton at the register. Sales UOM set to Unit (the retail majority); trade documents select Carton-12 per row. The rule filed with the register: the carton price and the factor are maintained together.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the lists (Chapter 4).</b> Two selling lists, matching the customer group design from module 1: <b>Standard Selling</b> (retail counters) and <b>Trade</b> (chain pharmacies, institutions, distributors — the module 1 chain resolves here through its group). No per-customer lists; the architecture note records the rejection with the reason.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the prices loaded (Chapter 5).</b> The approved price file loads per list, with per-UoM rows where trade sells cartons: PARA-500 carrying its unit rate on Standard Selling, and its unit and Carton-12 rates on Trade. The verification sample drafts documents per list; the file and sample file with the review.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the quantity break as a rule (Chapter 6).</b> The trade volume decision — <b>from 50 units, the rate becomes ₦950</b> — is configured as a Pricing Rule: scope PARA-500 for the Trade customer groups, condition min qty 50, effect the rate, validity open, owner and reason in the register. The rules read's effect sample confirms both sides: the 50-unit draft row prices at ₦950; the 49-unit row prices at the list rate.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the tax classifications (Chapter 7).</b> The ruling table applies by class: the exempt pharmaceutical classes carry the exempt template; consumables carry standard-rated. The mixed-basket draft — pharmaceuticals and consumables on one document — taxes per line correctly, and the quarterly read's first pass returns clean.</p><p><b>Step 7 — the first update cycle (Chapter 5).</b> A supplier cost change triggers the month's pricing review: the affected items' new rates approved with the margin arithmetic, loaded on the effective date, verified by sample, filed. The price changes read reconciles the month's Item Price modifications to the approved file exactly — the fields-move-only-by-decision audit passing on its first live month.</p><p><b>The architecture evidence.</b> The register holds: the coding and naming conventions, the unit register with verified factors, the two-list architecture note, the approved price files with verifications, the pricing rules register with the one active rule, and the tax ruling table — the pricing system answerable from one set of documents.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in items and pricing, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the typed price.</b> Symptom: rates entered at document time because the list has no price; margins varying by operator; disputes with no reference. Correction: the completeness read run, missing prices loaded by the update procedure. Prevention: every sales item priced on its group's list, and document-time rates reserved for approved negotiated cases only.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the second item record.</b> Symptom: a selling-side duplicate created for a product the register holds; stock and sales history split. Correction: the merge remediation per the lifecycle rules. Prevention: the one-record principle — the commercial section is completed on the existing record, through the gate.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the wrong conversion factor.</b> Symptom: carton documents mis-converting; fractional balances; the carton price and per-unit price disagreeing silently. Correction: the factor corrected as a governed change with affected prices reviewed. Prevention: factors verified against physical product, and the factor-and-price-together rule.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the list per customer.</b> Symptom: dozens of lists, each a full price set; updates missing lists; maintenance consuming the pricing function. Correction: lists consolidated to the class architecture; customer specifics moved to rules or approved document rates. Prevention: one behavioural class, one list — and the annual architecture read.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unregistered rule.</b> Symptom: pricing rules accumulating without owners; overlaps pricing documents by accident; expired promotions still firing. Correction: the register rebuilt, orphan rules disabled, priorities set explicitly. Prevention: every rule a register row at creation, end dates on promotions, and the monthly rules read with its effect sample.</p><p><b>Error 6 — tax by operator memory.</b> Symptom: per-document tax editing; mixed baskets taxed wholesale; exemptions applied on request. Correction: item classifications completed per the ruling table; document tax editing withdrawn per the matrix. Prevention: the item-side template by class at the gate, changes only by the accountant's dated ruling, and the quarterly read.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the commercial field walk with the code and naming conventions; operate the UoM table with verified factors and the sales unit; design the class-based list architecture and state what lists are not; maintain Item Prices through the governed update with the changes and completeness reads; build Pricing Rules with scope, condition, effect, and the register; and apply item tax classifications by ruling with the boundary respected.</p><p><b>Module rule 2: a price is a decision with a record — items selling from their one register record, units converting by verified factors, prices living on class lists and moving only by the governed update, conditions expressed as registered Pricing Rules, and tax treatment stated per the accountant's ruling — never a figure typed at document time.</b> Module 3 covers quotations.</p>"),
]

